Milo was a small gray cat with one white paw and a habit of watching the world from the windowsill. He lived in a quiet house at the end of Maple Lane, where the days passed slowly and the biggest excitement was usually a butterfly landing on the sill.

One morning, a bright blue feather drifted through the open window and landed at his paws. It shimmered like a piece of sky. Milo sniffed it, then picked it up in his teeth. As soon as he did, the feather glowed softly, and a tiny voice whispered, “Follow me.”

Milo looked around. No one was there.

He followed the feather anyway.

It floated ahead of him through the garden, past the tomato plants, and under the loose board in the fence. Beyond the fence was a path Milo had never noticed before, winding between tall trees and glowing mushrooms. The air smelled like rain and warm bread.

The feather led him to a stone bridge over a stream that sparkled with silver light. On the other side sat a fox wearing a tiny red scarf.

“You’re late,” the fox said.

Milo blinked. “I didn’t know I was expected.”

“The Moonberry is missing,” the fox said. “Without it, the forest can’t wake up.”

Milo had never been asked to do anything important before. But the forest looked dim and sleepy, and he wanted to help.

Together, they followed the feather deeper into the woods. It led them to a hollow tree where a crow sat guarding a glowing fruit shaped like a crescent moon.

“I found it first,” the crow said proudly.

Milo sat down and washed his paw. “It’s not yours,” he said. “The forest needs it.”

The crow tilted his head. “What will you give me?”

Milo thought for a moment. Then he pushed the blue feather toward the crow. “This can fly anywhere. You can carry it and see the whole sky.”

The crow’s eyes widened. He picked up the feather, and it lifted him gently into the air. He circled once, delighted, then dropped the Moonberry at Milo’s paws.

Milo carried it back to the fox, who placed it on a stone pedestal in the center of the clearing. Light spread through the trees, flowers opened, and birds began to sing.

The fox bowed. “Thank you, brave cat.”

Milo purred. “I was only following the feather.”

When he returned home, the window was still open, and the afternoon sun was warm on the sill. Milo curled up and closed his eyes, smiling.

He was just a cat again.

