He imagined it often. He imagined the creek that ran behind the neighbor's property, the one Eleanor mentioned when she complained about the damp smell in autumn. He imagined the woods beyond the creek, dark and tangled, full of creatures he had never seen. He imagined the hill at the end of the street, where the wind blew harder and the stars seemed closer. He imagined all of it, and none of it, because he had never been there.

Then came the evening of the open door.

It was a Tuesday in late October, the kind of evening where the air turned sharp and the sky bruised itself into shades of violet and deep indigo. Eleanor had been carrying a basket of laundry to the back porch and, distracted by a phone call, had left the door propped open with a brick. She went inside, forgot, and the door swung gently on its hinges, breathing cold air into the warm kitchen.

Barnaby was sitting on the kitchen tile, watching the steam rise from his water bowl, when he felt it. A draft. A whisper of wind carrying scents he had never encountered: wet earth, crushed leaves, the musky tang of fox, the distant sweetness of woodsmoke from a chimney three houses down. His pupils dilated until his eyes were almost entirely black. His tail, which had been resting lazily on the tile, lifted and began to sway.

